\section{The concept ledger}

The single source of truth for what exists and what is planned --- the
literate backlog. Lifecycle: \texttt{planned → stubbed → implemented → stable}.
The AI reads this to orient before touching code; it is the map of the
\emph{design}, complementing \texttt{weft -m}, the map of the \emph{code}.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lllll}
\toprule
Concept & Status & Depends on & Stage & Chapter \\
\midrule
<concept-a> & stubbed  & ---        & 1 & \S4 \\
<concept-b> & stubbed  & <concept-a>& 1 & \S5 \\
<concept-c> & planned  & <concept-b>& 2 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Seam contracts}

The chunks at the boundaries between concepts, named but empty. Naming the
seams before filling bodies fixes the interfaces so no one improvises them.
\texttt{weft --lint} will flag these as unused until the assembly wires them —
that is the expected signal of ``designed, not yet built''.

